WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.450
Hello everybody this is Peter Cooper from
the NCBI.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:08.720
With me today is Beverly Underwood from the
GenBank submission staff.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.470
Today we're going to talk about 16S GenBank
submissions.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:18.970
The slides for today should be available to
you in the handout section in the right-hand

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.980
side of the screen, you can download them
there if you want to.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:28.630
They are also available at the FTP site in
the Materials Q&amp;A directory; that compressed

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:29.970
URL will take you there.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.620
Now I will turn it over to Beverly.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.890
Thank you Peter and Welcome.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:42.150
Today we will show you how to submit your
16S ribosomal RNA data to NCBI.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:47.340
You will learn the fastest route to submitting
and receiving Accession numbers for 16S ribosomal

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.340
RNA sequences.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.680
We will show you where to access this faster
submission tool.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.890
We will demonstrate a submission
Help you understand this submission process

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.330
and
Provide you with a pre-submission checklist

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.199
to help your submission go more smoothly.

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:06.350
We will start right away with the demonstration.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.090
This is the link to the submission tool.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.700
(https://submit.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/subs/genbank)
You need to use your NCBI login or register

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.640
for a free NCBI account if you do not yet
have one.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.249
OK, we are now going to the Submission Portal
using the link in the slides.

00:01:19.249 --> 00:01:24.749
First, I need to check that I am logged in.

00:01:24.749 --> 00:01:35.909
I am not logged in, so I will click the login
button to enter my NCBI username and password

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:37.039
and click sign in.

00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:41.349
If you do not yet have a NCBI log in, you
can register for one by clicking on “register

00:01:41.349 --> 00:01:45.810
for a free NCBI account” and following the
steps on the page.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.429
So we are now logged in.

00:01:48.429 --> 00:01:51.729
This is the GenBank Submission Portal.

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:57.020
You can click on “Short Description and
brief instructions to read brief descriptions

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:02.859
of what you can submit in this tool and the
requirements for these submission types.

00:02:02.859 --> 00:02:07.739
Below that are submissions you may have already
made.

00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:12.620
To start a new submission, click the new submission
button.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.290
We have now started a new submission.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.930
At the top of the page is your SUB number.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.790
This SUB number is not an accession number.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:25.349
You should only use this number for tracking
and corresponding with NCBI until accessions

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:27.090
are assigned.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.500
This is the submitter page, this page has
your contact information.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.450
The address on this page will be displayed
in your final GenBank record.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.500
Throughout this talk, I may refer to a GenBank
record as a flatfile.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.459
For this webinar, these mean the same thing.

00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:48.239
If you are a new user, you will need to fill
out the form, making sure you have filled

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:50.900
in all the fields with an asterisk.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:54.450
If you are a return user, this form should
be filled out already with the information

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.319
you previously provided.

00:02:56.319 --> 00:03:04.049
Note that if you are not using a United States
address, the state/province field is optional

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:07.890
and you can leave this field blank.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.620
Review the information and make changes, as
needed.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.090
Again, this address will be displayed in the
final GenBank record.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:18.290
I have looked over everything on this page
and since my information is correct, I will

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:19.719
click continue.

00:03:19.719 --> 00:03:25.349
For this demonstration, we are submitting
prokaryotic 16S ribosomal RNA sequences, so

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:32.810
I will select small subunit rRNA only (16S
rRNA).

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:37.150
But you can see the other submission types
that you can submit through this tool listed

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.150
here.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.560
We may have webinars on those in the future.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.239
Below the submission types, is a free text
field for a submission title.

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:47.150
This field is optional.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:50.810
You may use the submission title to help you
track your submission.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:56.260
The submission title is not displayed in the
final GenBank record and is for your use only.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.900
For this demonstration, I will leave this
field blank.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.719
I am done with this page, I will click continue.

00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:07.529
Now we are on the Sequencing technology page.

00:04:07.529 --> 00:04:11.939
Select the method you used to obtain the sequences
from the options listed on this page or if

00:04:11.939 --> 00:04:17.139
your method is not listed, select Other and
type the method you used in the provided text

00:04:17.139 --> 00:04:18.639
box.

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:22.000
For this demonstration, I will select Sanger.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.300
Note if you used one of these other methods,
you will need to indicate if you assembled

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:34.800
the sequences and you will be prompted for
the name of the assembly program and version.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.480
Now we are on the Sequences page.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:42.139
First you need to select when you want the
sequences released to the public nucleotide

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:43.389
database.

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:49.370
If you want the sequences to be publicly available
as soon as possible, click release immediately.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:53.390
If you are still working on a publication
and do not want the sequences available for

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:57.199
some time, select release on a specified date.

00:04:57.199 --> 00:05:09.370
For this demonstration I will select to release
on a specified date and select April 29, 2017.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:14.520
Next you are asked if a chimera check program
was used to check the sequences.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.349
Chimeric sequences are artifact sequences
formed by the incorrect joining of two or

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:20.680
more biological sequences.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:25.759
Chimeric sequences are commonly formed during
laboratory procedures to isolate 16S ribosomal

00:05:25.759 --> 00:05:26.759
RNA.

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:32.919
There are publicly available chimera check
programs designed to identify chimeric sequences.

00:05:32.919 --> 00:05:37.289
We highly encourage you to perform a chimera
check of your sequences and remove chimeric

00:05:37.289 --> 00:05:40.880
sequences from your file prior to submitting.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:44.949
If you did this analysis, you should check
Yes and provide the name and version of the

00:05:44.949 --> 00:05:47.680
program you used.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.220
For this demonstration, I will select No.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:55.039
Please note that all submitted 16S sequences
will be screened for chimeras, so your submission

00:05:55.039 --> 00:05:59.090
will be delayed if chimeric sequences found
in the file.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:05.310
There is a link in the submission checklist
provided to you which discusses chimera detection.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:10.599
Next it is asked if your sequences are from
a pure-cultured or uncultured source.

00:06:10.599 --> 00:06:14.940
You may be wondering what these options mean
so I will describe some scenarios to help

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:16.370
explain these.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:21.180
A pure-cultured strain would be if you isolated
strains on an agar plate and then isolated

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.919
the 16S rRNA from the genome of an individual
strain on the plate.

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:30.370
An example of an uncultured sample would be
if you extracted DNA directly from a mixed

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:36.449
environmental sample, like a soil or gut sample,
and then amplified and sequenced 16S from

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:39.180
the mixed DNA sample.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:45.510
If you extracted 16S rRNA from a mixed sample,
then introduced the 16S rRNA into a laboratory

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:50.820
E coli strain, uncultured is still the appropriate
selection.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.650
I will select pure culture for my demonstration.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.090
Now it is time to provide the sequences file.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:06.580
But first, let us quickly look at a FASTA
file of multiple sequences.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:12.610
If you have multiple 16S sequences to submit,
put all of the sequences into one FASTA file,

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:13.849
like this example.

00:07:13.849 --> 00:07:17.960
It is very important to correctly format the
FASTA file.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:22.629
Each sequence in the FASTA file has a definition
line, which is marked by use of the greater

00:07:22.629 --> 00:07:26.449
than sign, &gt;, this is followed by the sequence
ID.

00:07:26.449 --> 00:07:31.470
In this file, I used the strain identifiers
as the sequence IDs.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:36.490
You may use your laboratory identifiers, such
as the strain or clone ID, for the sequence

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:41.289
IDs, but you may also use Seq1, Seq2, etc.

00:07:41.289 --> 00:07:46.160
The sequence IDs must be unique and must be
less than 25 characters.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.330
There are limits on what characters you can
use for the sequence IDs.

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:55.949
The checklist made available to you with this
webinar contains a link to FASTA Format help.

00:07:55.949 --> 00:08:00.289
You may also access it by clicking on the
help link on the sequences page.

00:08:00.289 --> 00:08:04.960
Here, "Help on FASTA file."

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.759
Now I will upload my FASTA file using the
choose file button.

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:15.680
Depending on your web browser, the button
may read “Browse” instead of choose file.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:21.599
Notice after I select the file a progress
bar appears to indicate the progression of

00:08:21.599 --> 00:08:22.930
file upload.

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:26.159
You will see something similar when files
are validated.

00:08:26.159 --> 00:08:30.380
Larger files take longer to both upload and
validate.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:34.510
The file is uploaded and I have filled out
all the fields on the page, so I will click

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:35.950
continue.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:40.630
If you miss a required field, you will get
a red error with directions on what you missed.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:45.810
After clicking continue, the file is validated
and the sequences are checked for some common

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:50.460
problems like vector contamination and chimeric
sequences.

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:55.970
If your sequences have these issues, you will
get notification on the sequences page.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:09:01.850
After your sequences pass validation, you
will be prompted for source information on

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.400
the Source Modifiers page.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.620
This page is where you will provide your source
metadata.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.590
Let’s look over this page briefly.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:15.200
There are short instructions and an example
source modifiers table which shows the type

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:18.140
of information collected here.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:22.720
The required information for your type of
submission is listed, but you may provide

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:27.810
more source information to make your submission
more informative to database users.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:32.280
A complete list of valid source modifiers
and descriptions may be found by clicking

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:38.410
on More help on source modifiers on the Source
Modifiers page.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:42.660
You may obtain a table template to provide
the source information by clicking on Download

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:45.220
Source Modifier Template.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:50.290
You can save this file to your computer and
open it for editing.

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:54.700
The table can be filled out in a text editor
or a spreadsheet program.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:10:00.970
If you use a spreadsheet program, make sure
you save the file type as Text (tab-delimited).

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:05.560
Instructions for using a spreadsheet program
can be found at the source modifiers link

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:08.470
that I pointed to earlier.

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:12.660
This is the source modifiers file I have already
prepared.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.980
The top row is the header row with the source
modifier labels.

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:19.970
Below the header row are the corresponding
data rows.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:25.460
The table contains the required information
for a cultured prokaryotic submission, which

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:30.930
includes sequence IDs, organism names, and
unique strains.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:36.040
I have also provided additional isolation-source
and country information.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:41.950
The organism is the scientific name of the
organism which provided the sequenced genetic

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:42.950
material.

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:46.770
It can be the genus species name, but does
not have to be that specific if you do not

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:48.280
know it.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:53.710
The strain is the alpha-numeric sample code
you use in your laboratory for this strain.

00:10:53.710 --> 00:11:00.390
In this file, pme07-01, and these other codes,
are a strain codes.

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:06.180
The isolation-source describes the local environment
where the sample was obtained.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:11.060
The country is the location where this organism
or sequenced sample was obtained.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:14.750
Additional information about the location
within the country may be included after the

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:19.700
country name and a colon.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:25.020
A link to these source modifier descriptions
is in the checklist provided to you with this

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:26.020
webinar.

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:32.120
All of the values in this table are separated
by a tab, so this is a tab-delimited table.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:44.400
I will now upload this table using the choose
file button and click continue to validate.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:49.020
My source modifiers file contained a row with
a new organism name.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:53.670
Because it is not yet a recognized name, I
am receiving a warning that NCBI does not

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:55.960
recognize the name.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:59.500
If you receive this warning, you should check
the spelling to check for typos.

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:04.910
If are no typos in the names and you are submitting
a sequence from a new organism or an organism

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:09.200
not yet in the NCBI taxonomy, click continue.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:13.380
If there is a typo, follow the directions
in the warning, correct the names in your

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:17.380
file and upload a new file for validation.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:22.080
Uploading a new file will overwrite the previous
file.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:27.880
The name in my demonstration is correctly
spelled so I will click continue.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:30.360
We are now on the references page.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:36.170
Everything you include on this page will appear
in the final GenBank record or flatfile.

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:38.440
At the top are the sequence authors.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:44.230
The sequence authors are the names of people
who helped with generating the sequences.

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:48.980
Names need to be entered with the first name
or given name first followed by the last name

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:49.980
or family name.

00:12:49.980 --> 00:13:02.760
So I will type my name, Beverly Underwood
and my colleague’s name Peter Cooper.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:07.320
Notice as I was typing a flatfile preview
appeared on the right.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.670
This is a display for you to review – it
displays how the names will appear in the

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.430
flatfile or final GenBank record.

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:19.860
If a name in the preview does not look correct
or how you want it to appear, you need to

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:21.500
correct it on the left.

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:25.880
You should pay attention to the preview and
correct the names as needed.

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:31.200
Now provide the publication where you have
or will discuss the data you are submitting.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:35.550
If you do not intend to publish a paper for
these sequences, but you want to make the

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:41.420
sequence data public, you may select unpublished
and leave the remaining fields blank.

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:46.240
If you will publish or have published, select
as appropriate, fill in the information and

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:47.240
click continue.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:53.860
Again, all of the information you provide
on this page will appear in the FF.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:56.890
Ok we are almost done!

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:02.310
The overview page, this is the final page
which displays all of the information you

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:04.720
provided to us in the previous pages.

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:10.560
The release date you selected is indicated
near the top of the summary.

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:16.010
The contact address for use in your records
is next, followed by the sequence authors,

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:20.060
publication information, and sequencing technology.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:26.070
The unprocessed files you uploaded are provided
here along with a feature table file that

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:28.950
was made from the files you provided.

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:35.110
A summary of what we have received as well
as a list of any processing that was done

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:41.030
to your fasta file, such as trimming vector,
removing sequences will be summarized in reports

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:42.030
here.

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:48.790
If nothing was done, the report will state
the number of sequences received and the sequence

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:50.060
length range.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:54.470
Processed FASTA and source files that will
be used to make your GenBank records are listed

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:55.470
last.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:15:00.970
If any sequences were removed or trimmed because
of vector or chimera, these files will contain

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:01.970
those edits.

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:02.970
Review this page it and if you have corrections,
go back using the tabs at the top to correct

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:03.970
the data.

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:08.310
After you have reviewed the information, click
Submit.

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:11.800
Congratulations we have now submitted data
to NCBI!

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:15.480
Im going to go back to my slides now.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:18.350
So what happens after we click Submit?

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:23.380
After you click submit on the overview page,
your submission will go into automated processing

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:27.050
where it is further evaluated for errors such
as those listed on this slide.

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:33.770
During this time, the status in Submission
Portal will either be “Queued” or “Processing”.

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:37.660
I’ll pause for a few seconds to give you
time to read this slide.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:44.420
If there are no errors, the submission will
quickly receive Accession numbers and you

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:48.480
will receive an email with a link to portal
where you can download the final processed

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:50.200
flatfiles.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:54.280
The submission status is processed and there
is a green check mark.

00:15:54.280 --> 00:16:00.750
If there are errors you need to correct, you
will receive an email shortly after submission,

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:04.380
like this one, with a link to the submission.

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:07.810
Click on the link in the email to be directed
to your submission.

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:14.800
In portal the status is “Error” and a
report describing the problems is posted.

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:18.480
Click on the error report to read what the
problems are.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:22.750
You may click on the headers in the error
report for more information about the error.

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:26.410
So these headers are clickable.

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:30.740
Once you have the corrected FASTA file or
know how to correct the problem, click the

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:38.520
Fix button to correct the problems in the
submission, and you can submit to try again.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:42.510
We have prepared for you a pre-submission
checklist of information you need to collect

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:45.750
ahead of time to help your submission go smoothly.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:51.640
This checklist pdf is available as a handout
in the webinar materials area.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:58.070
The checklist also contains an example flatfile
with the associated submission dialog pages

00:16:58.070 --> 00:17:05.730
to show how the information you provide relates
to the forms in the submission dialogs and

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:06.730
to the flatfile.

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:10.689
Ribosomal RNA submissions received through
this tool will be processed faster than other

00:17:10.689 --> 00:17:11.769
routes.

00:17:11.769 --> 00:17:16.709
Larger submissions will take longer, but currently
median response time to Accession numbers

00:17:16.709 --> 00:17:23.639
with final processed records or Error Reporting
is less than a day, but maybe as soon as 10

00:17:23.639 --> 00:17:24.909
minutes after clicking submit.

00:17:24.909 --> 00:17:28.629
So we encourage you to use this tool to get
your data out faster.

00:17:28.629 --> 00:17:32.950
Thank you, I’ll turn this over to Peter
now.

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:34.110
Thank you Beverly.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:38.190
We have a couple of questions I think are
worth addressing for everybody.

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:42.830
The first one, has to do with the source organism
name.

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:47.759
What if the organism name is unknown, what
do you include in the source modifier table?

00:17:47.759 --> 00:17:51.059
I think you can address it in two ways.

00:17:51.059 --> 00:17:57.990
One, to include the most precise taxonomic
group you know, that is probably the right

00:17:57.990 --> 00:17:58.990
answer.

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:02.759
But if it is completely unknown, what would
you recommend they do in that case?

00:18:02.759 --> 00:18:05.940
If it is completely unknown.

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:09.909
Use unknown organism.

00:18:09.909 --> 00:18:13.240
We would get that and we may ask them more
questions.

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:19.750
If it is from an uncultured sample, and you
know it is bacterial, or archaeal, then you

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:25.440
can call it uncultured bacterium, if it is
cultured, and you really don't know, you can

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:28.960
just call it bacterium I hope that helps.

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:34.410
Okay, another question that is similar has
to do with this concept of operational taxonomic

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:36.070
units.

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:41.259
Can they submit an OTU file along with mapping
files, telling you what sequence goes with

00:18:41.259 --> 00:18:43.080
what OTU.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:49.380
I think this is one we want to spend a little
time and give this person an answer from our

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:53.330
taxonomists, so we will consult with them
and get back to you.

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:57.840
Okay, another question is, which software
do you recommend for looking for chimeras,

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:00.190
you mentioned one, I think.

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:02.300
There are a number of them available.

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:07.559
In submission portal now, we are using uchime
to check for chimeras.

00:19:07.559 --> 00:19:08.559
There is also DECIPHER.

00:19:08.559 --> 00:19:14.450
There are some publicly available programs
if you search the literature.

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:18.740
One last question, this has to do with updating
your sequences.

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:25.559
One question was about... the specific example
was, you submitted unpublished, then you wanted

00:19:25.559 --> 00:19:29.830
to add the publication later to the submission,
what do you need to do?

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:33.600
Okay, so we have an update page.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:35.460
Okay, great.

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:41.879
So there is a link which describes how to
format your updates, so follow the instructions

00:19:41.879 --> 00:19:48.750
on the page, it includes information on how
to change your publication status from unpublished

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:49.990
to published.

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:57.450
As well as updating your sequences, source
modifiers, feature information, all sorts

00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:58.570
of information.

00:19:58.570 --> 00:20:06.610
And you would email that to gb-admin@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:12.999
Just make sure it is formatted as described
on the update page, and include your accession

00:20:12.999 --> 00:20:14.919
numbers with your request.

00:20:14.919 --> 00:20:17.429
Okay, that's all the questions that we have
time for today.

00:20:17.429 --> 00:20:23.029
If there are any others in the questions pod
that we didn't get to, we'll write out the

00:20:23.029 --> 00:20:31.809
answers and make them available in a document
in the Materials directory that I mentioned.

00:20:31.809 --> 00:20:35.830
We will also send you a link to a survey monkey.

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:40.749
We'd like to get some feedback both about
the webinar and about the submission tool.

00:20:40.749 --> 00:20:43.720
Thanks very much and thank you for coming
today.

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:43.730
&gt;&gt; [Event Concluded]

